{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 46, :comments ["<b>This is the law of the beast and the fowl.</b> The entire verse seems superfluous. And in Torat Kohanim they said (Ḥulin 27b) that it comes to state the law of slaughter of the fowl, through the fact that Scripture placed the fowl between the beast and the fish, which are 'living creatures' (נפש החיה) — to say that it is half beast and half fish; therefore the fowl requires one sign, or its majority. And according to this, we find a sound reason why Scripture needed to exclude fish from slaughter, as it is written (Numbers 11:22): ״יֵאָסֵף״ — for whence would it enter one's mind that they require it, such that Scripture needed to exclude them? Rather, it is because Scripture needed to write 'fish of the sea' to place them between the beast and the fowl, it was concerned lest one err, thinking that the intent of Scripture was to equate the fowl and the fish with the beast, that all of them require slaughter of two signs; therefore it said of fish 'gathering' (אסיפה). And with this you will understand the intent of HaShem in the verse, that it comes only regarding the fowl, to require of it one sign as mentioned." "And if you say, if so, why was it necessary to mention here ״נֶפֶשׁ הַשּׁוֹרֶצֶת״ — that is, the insects (חגבים)? It may be answered that Scripture was concerned lest one err from the verse that excluded slaughter for fish, as it is written ״יֵאָסֵף״ etc. (Numbers 11:22), for it excluded only fish but not insects; therefore it arranged them after the fish, to say that they do not require slaughter according to the law of fish:" "<b>Thus is concluded the parashah of Shemini.</b>"], :book :leviticus, :voice :or-hachaim, :lang :en, :chapter 11, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T19:55:39.447141205Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}